  "@graph": [
    {
      "id": "spf:skills",
      "type": [
        "schema:DefinedTermSet",
        "spf:DataType",
        "skos:ConceptScheme"
      ],
      "Data Type Name": "Skills",
      "Data Type Description": "A competency in an area of expertise.",
      "Relationship to Skills": "Skills and other Skills are related in the following ways:\n1. Some skills are prerequisites that people build before they can pick up more advanced skills.\n2. Growing one skill often strengthens the related skills a person already has.",
      "Relationship to Practices": "Skills and Practices are related in the following ways:\n1. People need practices like shared ownership and psychological safety to use their skills well on a team.\n2. Practices shape how a person applies a skill with others, so the same skill lands differently depending on the practices behind it.",
      "Relationship to Projects": "Skills and Projects are related in the following ways:\n1. People use their skills to move a project forward and get the work done.\n2. Projects give people a real place to apply and prove their skills.",
      "Relationship to Duties": "Skills and Duties are related in the following ways:\n1. People need certain skills to take on a duty and handle its responsibilities.\n2. A duty defines which skills a person is expected to bring and keep growing.",
      "Relationship to Stakeholders": "Skills and Stakeholders are related in the following ways:\n1. People use their skills to understand and meet what stakeholders need.\n2. Stakeholders drive the demand for specific skills by shaping what the work requires.",
      "Relationship to Milestones": "Skills and Milestones are related in the following ways:\n1. People must have the right skills to reach a milestone in the work.\n2. Each milestone calls for a particular set of skills before a team can complete it.",
      "Relationship to Tools": "Skills and Tools are related in the following ways:\n1. People rely on their skills to use tools effectively.\n2. Tools extend a person's skills and usually take skill to operate well.",
      "Relationship to Activities": "Skills and Activities are related in the following ways:\n1. People apply their skills to carry out the day-to-day activities of the work.\n2. Doing activities over and over is how people build and sharpen their skills.",
      "Relationship to Deliverables": "Skills and Deliverables are related in the following ways:\n1. People use their skills to create the deliverables a team produces.\n2. Each deliverable calls for specific skills to make it well.",
      "Relationship to Job Functions": "Skills and Job Functions are related in the following ways:\n1. People qualify for a job function by having the skills it calls for.\n2. A job function points to the skills a person is expected to have.",
      "Relationship to Job Specializations": "Skills and Job Specializations are related in the following ways:\n1. A specialization is built on a focused set of skills a person grows deep in.\n2. The skills you choose to sharpen point you toward a specialization.",
      "Relationship to Company Types": "Skills and Company Types are related in the following ways:\n1. The skills a person has help them fit the way different company types work.\n2. Different company types lean on different skills, so the setting shapes which skills matter most.",
      "Relationship to Methodologies": "Skills and Methodologies are related in the following ways:\n1. People apply their skills within a methodology to guide how the work flows.\n2. A methodology calls for certain skills, like facilitation or planning, to run it well.",
      "Relationship to Workshops": "Skills and Workshops are related in the following ways:\n1. People bring and use their skills to take part in workshops.\n2. Workshops are a hands-on way for people to build and practice skills together.",
