      ],
      "Data Type Name": "Practices",
      "Data Type Description": "A set of beliefs and behaviors that drive successful teamwork.",
      "Relationship to Skills": "Practices and Skills are related in the following ways:\n1. Practices shape how a person applies a skill with others, so the same skill lands differently depending on the practices behind it.\n2. People need practices like shared ownership and psychological safety to use their skills well on a team.",
      "Relationship to Practices": "Practices and other Practices are related in the following ways:\n1. Practices reinforce one another, so growing one practice often lifts the others.\n2. A team's practices work as a set, and weakness in one can undercut the rest.",
      "Relationship to Projects": "Practices and Projects are related in the following ways:\n1. Practices set the tone for how a team works together to deliver a project.\n2. Projects are where a team's practices get tested under real pressure.",
      "Relationship to Duties": "Practices and Duties are related in the following ways:\n1. Practices guide how a person carries out a duty alongside teammates.\n2. A duty is carried out through practices that guide how a person works with others.",
      "Relationship to Stakeholders": "Practices and Stakeholders are related in the following ways:\n1. Practices like humility and active listening shape how a team includes and serves its stakeholders.\n2. Stakeholders are served better when a team practices humility, listening, and inclusion.",
      "Relationship to Milestones": "Practices and Milestones are related in the following ways:\n1. Practices help a team stay aligned and adaptable as they work toward a milestone.\n2. Reaching a milestone is smoother when the team leans on practices like adaptability and shared ownership.",
      "Relationship to Tools": "Practices and Tools are related in the following ways:\n1. Practices influence how openly and collaboratively a team uses its tools.\n2. How openly a team uses its tools reflects its practices.",
      "Relationship to Activities": "Practices and Activities are related in the following ways:\n1. Practices shape how people show up for shared activities, like listening and making space for others.\n2. Activities are where practices show up in how people work together.",
      "Relationship to Deliverables": "Practices and Deliverables are related in the following ways:\n1. Practices affect the quality of deliverables by shaping how the team collaborates to make them.\n2. The quality of a deliverable reflects how well the team practiced collaboration to make it.",
      "Relationship to Job Functions": "Practices and Job Functions are related in the following ways:\n1. Practices define the human, behavioral side of doing a job function well.\n2. A job function is done well when a person brings strong team practices to it.",
      "Relationship to Job Specializations": "Practices and Job Specializations are related in the following ways:\n1. Practices are the team habits a person brings no matter which specialization they pick.\n2. Every specialization gets stronger when the person behind it works with healthy practices.",
      "Relationship to Company Types": "Practices and Company Types are related in the following ways:\n1. Practices help a team stay healthy no matter which company type they work in.\n2. Every company type benefits from healthy team practices, whatever its structure.",
      "Relationship to Methodologies": "Practices and Methodologies are related in the following ways:\n1. Practices are the mindset that makes a methodology actually work, not just its steps.\n2. A methodology only works when the team lives out the practices behind it.",
      "Relationship to Workshops": "Practices and Workshops are related in the following ways:\n1. Practices are taught and strengthened through team workshops.\n2. Workshops are where teams learn and rehearse healthy practices.",
      "Examples of Practices": [
        {
