    {
      "id": "spf:duty",
      "type": [
        "schema:DefinedTermSet",
        "spf:DataType",
        "skos:ConceptScheme"
      ],
      "Data Type Name": "Duty",
      "Data Type Description": "A set of expected duties.",
      "Relationship to Skills": "Duties and Skills are related in the following ways:\n1. A duty defines which skills a person is expected to bring and keep growing.\n2. People need certain skills to take on a duty and handle its responsibilities.",
      "Relationship to Practices": "Duties and Practices are related in the following ways:\n1. A duty is carried out through practices that guide how a person works with others.\n2. Practices guide how a person carries out a duty alongside teammates.",
      "Relationship to Projects": "Duties and Projects are related in the following ways:\n1. Duties give a project clear ownership by setting who is responsible for what.\n2. Projects assign duties to people so the work has clear ownership.",
      "Relationship to Duties": "Duties and other Duties are related in the following ways:\n1. Duties overlap, so one person's duty often depends on another's.\n2. Duties are shared and traded across a team as the work shifts.",
      "Relationship to Stakeholders": "Duties and Stakeholders are related in the following ways:\n1. Duties include being accountable to the stakeholders the work serves.\n2. Stakeholders rely on people fulfilling their duties to get what they need.",
      "Relationship to Milestones": "Duties and Milestones are related in the following ways:\n1. Duties determine who owns the work needed to reach each milestone.\n2. Milestones show which duties need to be active at each stage of the work.",
      "Relationship to Tools": "Duties and Tools are related in the following ways:\n1. Each duty comes with the tools a person is expected to use.\n2. The tools a person reaches for are often set by the duty they hold.",
      "Relationship to Activities": "Duties and Activities are related in the following ways:\n1. A duty is made up of the activities a person is responsible for.\n2. Activities are grouped into the duties people take responsibility for.",
      "Relationship to Deliverables": "Duties and Deliverables are related in the following ways:\n1. A duty is measured by the deliverables the person is accountable for.\n2. Deliverables are what people are held accountable for within their duties.",
      "Relationship to Job Functions": "Duties and Job Functions are related in the following ways:\n1. A duty is a set of responsibilities that can sit within one or more job functions.\n2. A job function is made up of the duties a person is expected to own.",
      "Relationship to Job Specializations": "Duties and Job Specializations are related in the following ways:\n1. A specialization is a focused combination of the duties a person chooses to take on.\n2. The duties you own shape the specialization you grow into.",
      "Relationship to Company Types": "Duties and Company Types are related in the following ways:\n1. How a duty is defined can shift depending on the company type.\n2. A company type shapes what a given duty involves day to day.",
      "Relationship to Methodologies": "Duties and Methodologies are related in the following ways:\n1. Duties are shaped by the methodology the team follows.\n2. A methodology shapes the duties and roles people take on.",
      "Relationship to Workshops": "Duties and Workshops are related in the following ways:\n1. Duties are clarified in workshops where teams agree on who owns what.\n2. Workshops help teams clarify who owns which duties.",
