    {
      "id": "spf:stakeholder",
      "type": [
        "schema:DefinedTermSet",
        "spf:DataType",
        "skos:ConceptScheme"
      ],
      "Data Type Name": "Stakeholder",
      "Data Type Description": "A person who drives the needs of work.",
      "Relationship to Skills": "Stakeholders and Skills are related in the following ways:\n1. Stakeholders drive the demand for specific skills by shaping what the work requires.\n2. People use their skills to understand and meet what stakeholders need.",
      "Relationship to Practices": "Stakeholders and Practices are related in the following ways:\n1. Stakeholders are served better when a team practices humility, listening, and inclusion.\n2. Practices like humility and active listening shape how a team includes and serves its stakeholders.",
      "Relationship to Projects": "Stakeholders and Projects are related in the following ways:\n1. Stakeholders set the direction and success measures for a project.\n2. Projects exist to deliver value to their stakeholders.",
      "Relationship to Duties": "Stakeholders and Duties are related in the following ways:\n1. Stakeholders rely on people fulfilling their duties to get what they need.\n2. Duties include being accountable to the stakeholders the work serves.",
      "Relationship to Stakeholders": "Stakeholders and other Stakeholders are related in the following ways:\n1. Different stakeholders often have needs that must be balanced against each other.\n2. Stakeholders influence one another in how they shape the work.",
      "Relationship to Milestones": "Stakeholders and Milestones are related in the following ways:\n1. Stakeholders watch milestones as signs the work is progressing.\n2. Milestones give stakeholders clear signals of how the work is progressing.",
      "Relationship to Tools": "Stakeholders and Tools are related in the following ways:\n1. Stakeholders may request certain tools or need access to the team's outputs.\n2. Tools help teams share progress and outputs with stakeholders.",
      "Relationship to Activities": "Stakeholders and Activities are related in the following ways:\n1. Stakeholders shape which activities matter most by voicing their needs.\n2. Activities are chosen and prioritized based on stakeholder needs.",
      "Relationship to Deliverables": "Stakeholders and Deliverables are related in the following ways:\n1. Stakeholders receive and judge the deliverables a team produces.\n2. Deliverables are handed to stakeholders as the value of the work.",
      "Relationship to Job Functions": "Stakeholders and Job Functions are related in the following ways:\n1. Stakeholders interact with many different job functions on a team.\n2. Each job function interacts with stakeholders in its own way.",
      "Relationship to Job Specializations": "Stakeholders and Job Specializations are related in the following ways:\n1. Different specializations serve different stakeholders and their needs.\n2. What a stakeholder needs can call for a specific specialization on the team.",
      "Relationship to Company Types": "Stakeholders and Company Types are related in the following ways:\n1. Who the stakeholders are changes with the company type, such as clients at an agency.\n2. The company type determines who the stakeholders are, like clients at an agency.",
      "Relationship to Methodologies": "Stakeholders and Methodologies are related in the following ways:\n1. Stakeholders are kept involved through methodologies that value collaboration.\n2. A methodology defines how and when stakeholders stay involved.",
      "Relationship to Workshops": "Stakeholders and Workshops are related in the following ways:\n1. Stakeholders often join workshops to share their needs and build alignment.\n2. Workshops bring stakeholders in to share needs and build alignment.",
      "Relationship to Job Industries": "Stakeholders and Job Industries are related in the following ways:\n1. Stakeholders come from the industry the work is being done for.\n2. The industry shapes who the stakeholders are and what they expect.",
      "Read Me File": [
        {
          "name": "stakeholder.md",
          "url": "https://baserow-backend-production20240528124524339000000001.s3.amazonaws.com/user_files/YQEBt3nTW1HEH0CZW9OO1iMfc3ucHULt_bacc24f2060a951dd7ec5c0aafb81798f8095fe5c149d724ba09e9ea1a2c75a8.md"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "spf:milestone",
      "type": [
        "schema:DefinedTermSet",
        "spf:DataType",
        "skos:ConceptScheme"
      ],
      "Data Type Name": "Milestone",
      "Data Type Description": "A measured outcome of work progress.",
      "Relationship to Skills": "Milestones and Skills are related in the following ways:\n1. Each milestone calls for a particular set of skills before a team can complete it.\n2. People must have the right skills to reach a milestone in the work.",
      "Relationship to Practices": "Milestones and Practices are related in the following ways:\n1. Reaching a milestone is smoother when the team leans on practices like adaptability and shared ownership.\n2. Practices help a team stay aligned and adaptable as they work toward a milestone.",
      "Relationship to Projects": "Milestones and Projects are related in the following ways:\n1. Milestones break a project into clear stages of progress.\n2. Projects are broken into milestones that mark progress along the way.",
      "Relationship to Duties": "Milestones and Duties are related in the following ways:\n1. Milestones show which duties need to be active at each stage of the work.\n2. Duties determine who owns the work needed to reach each milestone.",
      "Relationship to Stakeholders": "Milestones and Stakeholders are related in the following ways:\n1. Milestones give stakeholders clear signals of how the work is progressing.\n2. Stakeholders watch milestones as signs the work is progressing.",
      "Relationship to Milestones": "Milestones and other Milestones are related in the following ways:\n1. Milestones build on each other, so one usually must be reached before the next.\n2. Progress at one milestone sets up the work for the ones that follow.",
      "Relationship to Tools": "Milestones and Tools are related in the following ways:\n1. Milestones often require specific tools to reach and to show progress.\n2. Tools help teams reach and track their milestones.",
      "Relationship to Activities": "Milestones and Activities are related in the following ways:\n1. Milestones are reached by completing the right activities in sequence.\n2. Completing the right activities is how a team reaches a milestone.",
      "Relationship to Deliverables": "Milestones and Deliverables are related in the following ways:\n1. Each milestone is marked by the deliverables the team produces at that stage.\n2. Deliverables mark when a milestone has been reached.",
      "Relationship to Job Functions": "Milestones and Job Functions are related in the following ways:\n1. Milestones call on different job functions at different stages.\n2. Different job functions lead at different milestones.",
      "Relationship to Job Specializations": "Milestones and Job Specializations are related in the following ways:\n1. Different milestones lean on different specializations to move the work forward.\n2. A specialization shows up most at the milestones where its focus matters.",
      "Relationship to Company Types": "Milestones and Company Types are related in the following ways:\n1. Which milestones matter can vary by company type and project.\n2. The company type can change which milestones a project focuses on.",
      "Relationship to Methodologies": "Milestones and Methodologies are related in the following ways:\n1. Milestones are organized by the methodology the team uses to plan work.\n2. A methodology organizes the work into milestones and cycles.",
      "Relationship to Workshops": "Milestones and Workshops are related in the following ways:\n1. Milestones are often planned and reviewed in team workshops.\n2. Workshops help teams plan for and review their milestones.",
