    {
      "id": "spf:activity",
      "type": [
        "schema:DefinedTermSet",
        "spf:DataType",
        "skos:ConceptScheme"
      ],
      "Data Type Name": "Activity",
      "Data Type Description": "A routine that people do together.",
      "Relationship to Skills": "Activities and Skills are related in the following ways:\n1. Doing activities over and over is how people build and sharpen their skills.\n2. People apply their skills to carry out the day-to-day activities of the work.",
      "Relationship to Practices": "Activities and Practices are related in the following ways:\n1. Activities are where practices show up in how people work together.\n2. Practices shape how people show up for shared activities, like listening and making space for others.",
      "Relationship to Projects": "Activities and Projects are related in the following ways:\n1. Activities are the day-to-day work that moves a project forward.\n2. Projects are made up of many activities the team carries out over time.",
      "Relationship to Duties": "Activities and Duties are related in the following ways:\n1. Activities are grouped into the duties people take responsibility for.\n2. A duty is made up of the activities a person is responsible for.",
      "Relationship to Stakeholders": "Activities and Stakeholders are related in the following ways:\n1. Activities are chosen and prioritized based on stakeholder needs.\n2. Stakeholders shape which activities matter most by voicing their needs.",
      "Relationship to Milestones": "Activities and Milestones are related in the following ways:\n1. Completing the right activities is how a team reaches a milestone.\n2. Milestones are reached by completing the right activities in sequence.",
      "Relationship to Tools": "Activities and Tools are related in the following ways:\n1. Activities are carried out using the team's tools.\n2. Tools are what people use to carry out many activities.",
      "Relationship to Activities": "Activities and other Activities are related in the following ways:\n1. Activities build on one another, with one often setting up the next.\n2. Some activities repeat in cycles as the work continues.",
      "Relationship to Deliverables": "Activities and Deliverables are related in the following ways:\n1. Activities are the steps that produce a deliverable.\n2. Deliverables are the result of completing a set of activities.",
      "Relationship to Job Functions": "Activities and Job Functions are related in the following ways:\n1. Activities are divided among different job functions.\n2. A job function is defined by the activities it is responsible for.",
      "Relationship to Job Specializations": "Activities and Job Specializations are related in the following ways:\n1. A specialization is known for the activities it carries out most.\n2. Doing the activities of a focus area over time is how a specialization grows.",
      "Relationship to Company Types": "Activities and Company Types are related in the following ways:\n1. Which activities a team does can vary by company type.\n2. The company type influences which activities a team spends time on.",
      "Relationship to Methodologies": "Activities and Methodologies are related in the following ways:\n1. Activities are organized and sequenced by the team's methodology.\n2. A methodology sequences the activities a team carries out.",
      "Relationship to Workshops": "Activities and Workshops are related in the following ways:\n1. A workshop is a structured kind of activity a team does together.\n2. A workshop is a structured group activity with a shared goal.",
