      "Relationship to Job Industries": "Activities and Job Industries are related in the following ways:\n1. Many activities are similar across industries, while some are specific to one.\n2. Everyday activities in an industry give people relevant experience.",
      "Read Me File": [
        {
          "name": "activity.md",
          "url": "https://baserow-backend-production20240528124524339000000001.s3.amazonaws.com/user_files/IzpzhqMtipyVh1cF3sKQJGvqXzAoS8Zd_d38c73c00a62791fede7aa21ae6037cef0fecf0ba1669d45242ed115dc01eafa.md"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "spf:deliverable",
      "type": [
        "schema:DefinedTermSet",
        "spf:DataType",
        "skos:ConceptScheme"
      ],
      "Data Type Name": "Deliverable",
      "Data Type Description": "Something provided as a result of a process.",
      "Relationship to Skills": "Deliverables and Skills are related in the following ways:\n1. Each deliverable calls for specific skills to make it well.\n2. People use their skills to create the deliverables a team produces.",
      "Relationship to Practices": "Deliverables and Practices are related in the following ways:\n1. The quality of a deliverable reflects how well the team practiced collaboration to make it.\n2. Practices affect the quality of deliverables by shaping how the team collaborates to make them.",
      "Relationship to Projects": "Deliverables and Projects are related in the following ways:\n1. Deliverables are the concrete results a project is judged by.\n2. Projects produce deliverables as the concrete results of the work.",
      "Relationship to Duties": "Deliverables and Duties are related in the following ways:\n1. Deliverables are what people are held accountable for within their duties.\n2. A duty is measured by the deliverables the person is accountable for.",
      "Relationship to Stakeholders": "Deliverables and Stakeholders are related in the following ways:\n1. Deliverables are handed to stakeholders as the value of the work.\n2. Stakeholders receive and judge the deliverables a team produces.",
      "Relationship to Milestones": "Deliverables and Milestones are related in the following ways:\n1. Deliverables mark when a milestone has been reached.\n2. Each milestone is marked by the deliverables the team produces at that stage.",
      "Relationship to Tools": "Deliverables and Tools are related in the following ways:\n1. Deliverables are created and refined using the team's tools.\n2. Tools are used to build and refine deliverables.",
      "Relationship to Activities": "Deliverables and Activities are related in the following ways:\n1. Deliverables are the result of completing a set of activities.\n2. Activities are the steps that produce a deliverable.",
      "Relationship to Deliverables": "Deliverables and other Deliverables are related in the following ways:\n1. Deliverables build on one another, with one often feeding into the next.\n2. One deliverable can be a building block for a larger one.",
      "Relationship to Job Functions": "Deliverables and Job Functions are related in the following ways:\n1. Different job functions own different deliverables.\n2. Each job function is known for the deliverables it produces.",
      "Relationship to Job Specializations": "Deliverables and Job Specializations are related in the following ways:\n1. Each specialization tends to produce its own kinds of deliverables.\n2. The deliverables a person makes are proof of the specialization they are building.",
      "Relationship to Company Types": "Deliverables and Company Types are related in the following ways:\n1. Some deliverables, like a statement of work, are tied to certain company types.\n2. Some deliverables are specific to a company type, like a pitch deck at a startup.",
      "Relationship to Methodologies": "Deliverables and Methodologies are related in the following ways:\n1. Which deliverables a team makes is shaped by its methodology.\n2. A methodology shapes which deliverables a team creates and when.",
      "Relationship to Workshops": "Deliverables and Workshops are related in the following ways:\n1. Many workshops are designed to produce a specific deliverable.\n2. A workshop often ends with a finished deliverable in hand.",
